  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">High Flyers - Tracking the Entrepreneurs</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The aim of the programme is to further understand what works when supporting High Flyers, especially underserved High Flyers. We will codify these learnings, enable ESOs to share in this knowledge and by institutionalising best practices across YBI. Therefore maximising the impact of High Flying entrepreneurs as they grow and scale their businesses, creating jobs and transforming communities.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The world in which young people operate their businesses is increasingly volatile and uncertain. The political and economic crisis in the past decades, and most recently the COVID 19 pandemic, has shown that small businesses, especially those led by women, youth, ethnic minorities, and migrants, have been hard hit the most. 
 
In lower-income countries such as Uganda, an average of 79% of small businesses reported a decrease in turnover2 (IFC data) and faced significant financial difficulties due to the economic impact of COVID-19.. In 2020, 50% of SMEs decreased their employment compared to 2019.3 
 
This situation has worsened unemployment rates and has particularly affected young people, especially those from developing countries. Due to the pandemic, youth employment dropped by 8.7% in 2020. The fall in employment was less severe for adults, registering at 3.7 %.4 This difference illustrates the pandemic&#x2019;s economic consequences for younger people and the imperative work that needs to be done in terms of job creation. 
 
SMEs play a significant role in most economies, particularly in developing countries. According to the World Bank, they represent 90% of businesses and more than 50% of employment worldwide.5 Looking at building stronger economies beyond the pandemic, strengthening youth founded small businesses must be at the centre of the strategies. They are vital vehicles and important contributors to job creation, economic and social inclusion, and resilience in the face of uncertainty and conflict.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This programme will directly build the capacities of the four implementing members, CAPYEI in Kenya, Cordaid in Uganda, Corporaci&#xF3;n Minuto de Dios in Colombia and Enterprise Uganda in Uganda, improve the practices of 8 ESOs from YBI&#x2019;s network and directly support a small group of High Flyers in 3 countries, Colombia, Kenya and Uganda.

High Flying entrepreneurs are defined as young people aged 18-35 who have been running a business for approximately 2 years with an annual average business revenue of US$2,000 &#x2013; US$5,000. High Flyers come from diverse backgrounds and it is integral everyone is given the opportunity to grow and scale a business. The programme will support a range of young people from disadvantaged backgrounds, including women, migrants and people in rural areas.</narrative>
  </description>
